{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 18,
  "title": "The bathroom",
  "description": "Name the things in a bathroom and say what you use them for.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "The bathroom has its own small set of nouns. Most of them end in -o and are masculine, and several of them are used with a piece of clothing or a room rather than on their own.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The nouns",
        "body": "El lavabo, el inodoro and el secador are all masculine. La ducha and la bañera are feminine. One of them is a phrase with its own article inside it: la pasta de dientes is a feminine noun with de dientes attached, and you say una pasta de dientes, never un pasta.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el lavabo",
            "en": "the sink"
          },
          {
            "es": "la ducha",
            "en": "the shower"
          },
          {
            "es": "la pasta de dientes",
            "en": "the toothpaste"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Saying what you use",
        "body": "You already know tener, so you can say what a person has and what they need. Para says what something is for: el peine es para el pelo, the comb is for the hair. In a shop you ask for it with un or una, and the noun tells you which one.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el peine es para el pelo",
            "en": "the comb is for the hair"
          },
          {
            "es": "tengo un champú",
            "en": "I have a shampoo"
          },
          {
            "es": "el secador es para el pelo",
            "en": "the hairdryer is for the hair"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
